WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.317
Here is a puzzle that catches almost every team once.

2
00:00:06.317 --> 00:00:07.534
Two pull requests.

3
00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:09.089
Both have a green tick.

4
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:12.281
Both merge, and Git reports no conflict.

5
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.094
And then main is broken.

6
00:00:14.094 --> 00:00:16.128
How can two greens make a red?

7
00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:20.391
Let's leave code alone for a minute, and walk into a restaurant kitchen.

8
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.633
Every cook here works from one house recipe book.

9
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.220
One Friday morning, two cooks each set out to improve it.

10
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.164
The first cook moves the spare stock out of the blue pot, into a new green pot.

11
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:38.640
Then they fix every recipe card that named the blue pot.

12
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.547
Careful work.

13
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:43.914
The second cook, working from the breakfast cards, writes a new soup.

14
00:00:43.914 --> 00:00:46.701
Its card says: take a ladle from the blue pot.

15
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:48.935
Each cook checks their own work.

16
00:00:48.935 --> 00:00:50.421
Both changes look fine.

17
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.868
At noon, both changes are in the book.

18
00:00:52.868 --> 00:00:55.136
The new soup reaches for the blue pot.

19
00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:57.761
And the pot is empty.

20
00:00:57.933 --> 00:00:59.212
Nobody was careless.

21
00:00:59.212 --> 00:01:03.749
Each check was honest, and the two changes never touched the same card.

22
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:08.698
The trouble lives in the combination, and nobody tasted the combination.

23
00:01:08.698 --> 00:01:10.088
The soup was perfect.

24
00:01:10.088 --> 00:01:11.676
For yesterday's kitchen.

25
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.409
Now let's map the kitchen onto GitHub, the website that keeps a team's shared copy of the code.

26
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.489
The recipe book is main, the branch of code everyone uses.

27
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:28.412
A cook's copy is a branch, a separate line of changes where you can work without touching main.

28
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.671
The page on the head chef's desk is a pull request.

29
00:01:31.671 --> 00:01:36.187
It asks to add your branch to main, so people and tests can check it first.

30
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:41.716
And the taster is the checks: automatic tests that show a green tick or a red cross.

31
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:49.988
Here is the same story in our lab app, hello-devops, a tiny web app that greets every visitor and counts the visits.

32
00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:55.773
Teammate A renames a function, count_visit, to record_visit, and fixes its one caller.

33
00:01:55.773 --> 00:01:57.805
That's the stock changing pots.

34
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.194
Teammate B adds a new web address, /hello/<name>, with a test.

35
00:02:02.194 --> 00:02:05.779
It calls count_visit, which was correct that morning.

36
00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:08.712
Pull request 12: three tests pass.

37
00:02:08.712 --> 00:02:11.408
Pull request 13: four tests pass.

38
00:02:11.408 --> 00:02:12.716
Two green ticks.

39
00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:13.641
Both merge.

40
00:02:13.641 --> 00:02:17.723
No conflict, because the branches changed different lines.

41
00:02:17.867 --> 00:02:19.926
Now the tests run on main.

42
00:02:19.926 --> 00:02:21.194
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:21.194 --> 00:02:22.303
Green, or red?

44
00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:24.797
Red.

45
00:02:24.797 --> 00:02:28.351
NameError: name count_visit is not defined.

46
00:02:28.351 --> 00:02:30.417
One failed, three passed.

47
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:30.941
Well.

48
00:02:30.941 --> 00:02:33.122
That's not the lunch we planned.

49
00:02:33.122 --> 00:02:36.871
The new address calls a function that no longer exists.

50
00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:41.081
Now that you've seen it, it gets a name: a semantic conflict.

51
00:02:41.081 --> 00:02:42.661
The text merged cleanly.

52
00:02:42.661 --> 00:02:43.977
The meaning did not.

53
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:47.532
Let's rewind to just before 13 merged.

54
00:02:47.532 --> 00:02:50.199
What did its green tick really promise?

55
00:02:50.199 --> 00:02:53.551
Most of us read it as: my branch works with main.

56
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:56.556
That's almost right, and the almost is the bug.

57
00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:57.321
Fact one.

58
00:02:57.321 --> 00:03:05.088
GitHub builds a temporary merge commit, your branch joined with the current main, and the checks test that combination.

59
00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:09.235
When pull request 13 was opened, main was at m1.

60
00:03:09.235 --> 00:03:10.805
So: green, with m1.

61
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:11.587
Fact two.

62
00:03:11.587 --> 00:03:16.286
The checks start only when something happens to the pull request itself.

63
00:03:16.286 --> 00:03:19.745
It is opened, it gets new commits, or it is reopened.

64
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:23.862
Now pull request 12 merges, and main moves on to m2.

65
00:03:23.862 --> 00:03:26.581
Is that an event on pull request 13?

66
00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:27.030
No.

67
00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.512
Nothing reruns.

68
00:03:28.512 --> 00:03:30.785
The tick still says m1.

69
00:03:30.933 --> 00:03:35.804
Honestly, that tick should come with a label: green, as of yesterday's main.

70
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.275
A tick is a photo of one moment.

71
00:03:38.275 --> 00:03:39.981
It is not a live camera.

72
00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:44.153
So the wish is simple: taste the soup in today's kitchen.

73
00:03:44.333 --> 00:03:49.425
A ruleset, a set of rules for a branch, has an option for exactly that.

74
00:03:49.425 --> 00:03:52.940
Require branches to be up to date before merging.

75
00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.231
Day 25 builds one.

76
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.162
Pull request 13 is now behind main, so it cannot merge yet.

77
00:03:58.162 --> 00:04:02.051
The Update branch button brings the new main into the branch.

78
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.988
That is a new commit on the pull request, so the checks run again, on B's branch plus m2.

79
00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:10.978
Red, on the pull request instead of on main.

80
00:04:10.978 --> 00:04:13.757
The merge is blocked, and main stays green.

81
00:04:13.933 --> 00:04:17.805
And if a break does slip through, undo it with a new commit.

82
00:04:17.805 --> 00:04:23.483
A merge commit has two parents, so -m 1 tells revert to keep the first one, main's side.

83
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.535
Should every repository turn this rule on?

84
00:04:27.535 --> 00:04:28.431
Mostly, yes.

85
00:04:28.431 --> 00:04:29.775
But it has a cost.

86
00:04:29.933 --> 00:04:35.209
On a busy repository, people keep updating their branches and rerunning the checks.

87
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.512
When many pull requests land every hour, teams use a merge queue.

88
00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:45.205
GitHub tests main plus the pull requests ahead of yours, and merges only when that passes.

89
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:50.421
And the cheapest guard costs nothing: small branches, merged within a day or two.

90
00:04:50.421 --> 00:04:54.203
Less time waiting means less time for main to move under you.

91
00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:56.429
So here's the moral.

92
00:04:56.429 --> 00:05:03.552
A pull request's checks test your branch merged into main, as main was the last time the pull request changed.

93
00:05:03.733 --> 00:05:06.825
Two honest checks can still miss the combination.

94
00:05:06.825 --> 00:05:08.781
So taste it in today's kitchen.

95
00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:09.764
Quick check.

96
00:05:09.764 --> 00:05:12.462
Your pull request went green yesterday.

97
00:05:12.462 --> 00:05:15.367
This morning, a teammate merged into main.

98
00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.097
Has your check run again?

99
00:05:17.097 --> 00:05:18.204
Pause and think.

100
00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:20.608
No.

101
00:05:20.608 --> 00:05:24.484
Nothing happened to your pull request, so nothing reran.

102
00:05:24.484 --> 00:05:27.737
Press Update branch, or let the ruleset insist.
